  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 215 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSaturday through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis:\nWarm weather continues into Saturday (highs in the low 60s) with\na south breeze. A period of rain showers is expected (60-70%\nchances) at some point between Saturday afternoon and Sunday\nmorning ahead of and along a passing cold front; exact timing\ndepends on when the front passes through. A sharp cooling trend\nthen kicks in behind the front, plunging daytime highs to the\nlow 40s next Monday and Tuesday. Lows in the 20s expected for\ninland areas early next week.\n\nDiscussion:\nSaturday morning features a high-amplitude trough over the Rocky\nMountains and low pressure tracking northeastward into Ontario\nCanada. Surface high pressure (the same one from earlier this\nweek) manages to linger over the northeastern CONUS states, with\na tight pressure gradient over WI driving a south breeze.\nContinued WAA allows daytime highs to remain in the low 60s\nSaturday despite approaching cloud cover.\n\nThe greatest differences we're seeing between ECMWF and GFS\nmodel guidance this weekend is the timing of the cold front\n(and associated precip ahead of and along it). Rain could \narrive as early as Saturday afternoon or hold off until Sunday\nmorning. The latest model trends have been to delay the arrival\nof the front slightly, hence precip chances for Saturday\nafternoon are falling and Sunday morning is on the rise.\n\nWinds veer NW and become breezy again behind the front (either\nSunday morning or afternoon). Exact front timing will determine\nSunday's high temperatures, for now we're going with upper 40s\nas a 'middle ground' solution. A delayed front could trend that\nwarmer. Highs temps then hit the low 40s (perhaps even 40\ndegrees flat) for Monday and Tuesday. Lows will be in the 20s\nearly next week, perhaps locally warmer by Lake MI due to some\nweak onshore flow. Likely dry weather Monday and Tuesday as high\npressure settles into the Great Lakes region. \n\nSheppard",
